   "At first it was only a smudge on the horizon, a grey thumbprint against the blue. By noon the smudge had become a wall. Smoke rolled down the valley in slow, choking waves, and behind it the ridge glowed an angry orange, as though the mountain itself were bleeding. Ash drifted down like dirty snow, settling on the washing line, the windscreen, the back of my hand.",
   "We did not pack so much as grab. Mum snatched the photo albums from the shelf; Dad flung water bottles into the boot; my sister clutched the cat, which yowled and clawed at her arms. Nobody spoke. The road out of the village was a single slow snake of headlights, crawling through the smoke, and every time I looked back the orange glow had crept a little closer, patient and hungry.",
